UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 24, Lesson 3: Language of online texts', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are high. Keep threads cohesive.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRevise an ambiguous message into a clearer online register for a group chat about a group project.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise.\n\nCHECK\nUsing slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRewrite five posts for clarity/cohesion; annotate emoji/hashtag functions.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are high. Keep threads cohesive.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and vocabulary","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECB-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rewrite five posts for clarity/cohesion; annotate emoji/hashtag functions. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise. Reasoning: I used online register by attending to emoji function rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins. Correction: Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise. Why: the correction restores accurate use of online register.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating online register on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with online register, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise. This matches the lesson skill for online register.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise.","Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Revise an ambiguous message into a clearer online register for a group chat about a group project. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are…","Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise.","Careful use of emoji function with clear evidence.","Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising online register?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named emoji function, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the emoji function used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising online register. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries online register.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show online register and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"When the ferry horn blasted, Omar gripped the railing and laughed.\" Which choice best uses online register from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise.","Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about online register?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise emoji function?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about online register.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of online register to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating online register on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise. Reasoning: I used online register by attending to emoji function rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with online register, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using slang that excludes or insults. Inclusive clarity wins. Correction: Strong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise. Why: the correction restores accurate use of online register.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w24-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise online register.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use online register with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify emoji function and explain why it matters.","I can practise thread cohesion independently on a new prompt.","I can notice misread risk needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are high. Keep threads cohesive. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are high. Keep threads cohesive.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about online register?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n''Fine.'' can read as angry; ''Fine 🙂'' softens—but do not rely on emoji for serious apologies. Thread: problem → details → link → ask.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRevise an ambiguous message into a clearer online register for a group chat about a group project.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Tone unambiguous; still concise.","explain":"Online language mixes registers: abbreviations, emojis as tone markers, hashtags as topic labels. Misreads happen without tone cues—clarify when stakes are high. Keep threads cohesive.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explai